    "title": "<p class=\"TableTitle\"><strong>Table&nbsp;4.04</strong> Immunohistochemical markers for the most common bone metastases</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Andrew M. Bellizzi",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 99.52%; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"99%\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Primary tumour</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Cytoplasmic or membranous markers</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Nuclear markers<sup>a</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Lung (adenocarcinoma)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Napsin&nbsp;A (80%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">TTF1 (80%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Prostate</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">PSA, PAP</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">NKX3-1</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Breast</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">GCDFP-15 (25%), mammaglobin (50%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">GATA3, TRPS1, SOX10 (60%; triple negative)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Small cell carcinoma<sup>b</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Chromogranin&nbsp;A (&ge;&nbsp;50%), synaptophysin (&ge;&nbsp;60%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">INSM1, RB1 (loss), POU2F3 (15%; in general neuroendocrine marker weak-to-negative tumours)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma<sup>c</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">High-molecular-weight keratins (e.g. 34&beta;E12, CK5/6)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">p40</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 6; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Kidney</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cadherin&nbsp;16 (&ge;&nbsp;75%), napsin&nbsp;A (papillary 80%, clear cell 40%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">PAX8</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 7; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Gastro-oesophageal junction/stomach</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cadherin&nbsp;17 (&ge;&nbsp;50%), CK20 (&ge;&nbsp;40%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CDX2 (&ge;&nbsp;50%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 8; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Colorectum</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cadherin&nbsp;17, CK20</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CDX2, SATB2 (85%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 9; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Pancreatobiliary tree</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cadherin&nbsp;17 (30%), CK20 (25%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">SMAD4<sup>d </sup>(loss; 40%), CDX2 (30%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 10; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Urothelial carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">34&beta;E12, uroplakin-2 (67%), CK5/6 (50%), CK20 (50%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">p40 (85%), GATA3 (80%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 11; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">HepPar1 (&ge;&nbsp;80%), ARG1<sup>d </sup>(&ge;&nbsp;75%), GPC3 (50% in well-differentiated to 70% in poorly differentiated), albumin immunohistochemistry</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">None</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 12; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Thyroid</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Thyroglobulin, cadherin&nbsp;16 (follicular 60%, papillary 10%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">PAX8, TTF1</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 13; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>NET</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Chromogranin&nbsp;A, synaptophysin</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">INSM1</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 14; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Kappa and lambda light chains, CD79a, CD138</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">IRF4 (MUM1)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 15; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.52%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Melanoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">S100<sup>d</sup>, melan&nbsp;A, and HMB45 (85% in epithelioid, 45% in spindle cell)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 37.24%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"37%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">SOX10</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">ARG1, arginase-1; GPC3, glypican-3; NET, neuroendocrine tumour.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Notes: The frequency of positivity approaches 100% unless otherwise noted. <sup>a</sup>Nuclear markers are especially sensitive to decalcification; negative results should be interpreted with caution. <sup>b</sup>Immunohistochemistry can only distinguish tumours of visceral origin (TTF1, ASCL1) from those of cutaneous/Merkel cell origin (CK20, neurofibromin [NF1], SATB2, Merkel cell polyomavirus LT, POU4F3). <sup>c</sup>Although not specific for squamous cell carcinoma (e.g. also frequent in neuroendocrine carcinoma [NEC]), strong p16 staining (e.g. &ge;&nbsp;70% nuclear and cytoplasmic staining of at least moderate intensity) supports an association with high-risk HPV and oropharyngeal or anogenital origin. <sup>d</sup>Nuclear and cytoplasmic staining.</p>",
